\section*{Quadratic sensitivity versus block sensitivity}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 132; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that total Boolean functions can have block sensitivity exceeding sensitivity to a fixed exponent above two, disproving a universal quadratic relation.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For a total Boolean function, let $s(f)$ be its maximum number of sensitive individual coordinates and $\operatorname{bs}(f)$ its maximum number of pairwise disjoint sensitive blocks. The claim states
\[
 \forall C>0\ \exists n\ge1\ \exists f:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}:\quad
 f\text{ nonconstant},\quad\operatorname{bs}(f)>C s(f)^2.
\]
Its explicit family has, for every integer $d\ge1$,
\[
 \operatorname{bs}(f)/s(f)^2\ge 2^d/[4(d+2)^2].
\]

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 132; 2026-09-25), nested predicates are composed using labeled tournament rows. Strong child acceptance and weaker outgoing rejection requirements keep one-bit changes scarce, while disjoint zero-input blocks multiply through the recursion; a fixed composition converts the growing ratio into an exponent gap.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 132; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 132; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: total Boolean functions can have block sensitivity exceeding sensitivity to a fixed exponent above two, disproving a universal quadratic relation.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{SensitivitySeparation.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Paper320.quantitative\_separation}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

